        <body>
            <div xml:id="d672">

                <pb facs="0788" n="776" sameAs="frus:frus1942v01/pg776"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.248/805: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Ireland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Dublin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-12-12T11:00:00+01:00">December 12, 1942—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 11:25 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>253. Greatly appreciate your kind approval reference your 157, December 10, 6
                    p.m. Pursuant to your suggestion in your 154 of December 8, 7 p.m., I am
                    accepting a letter from Walshe of External Affairs which is a memorandum of the
                    conversation reported in my 239 of December 1, 2 p.m., instead of an exchange of
                    notes as an <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> of our understanding. This
                    will be forwarded by air courier as soon as received.</p>
                <p>My reason for appearing to lay emphasis on your reluctance to accept internment
                    as [a matter?] of course for groundings during operational flights was mainly to
                    induce construction of doubtful cases as nonoperational. The Irish Government
                    wishes to avoid trouble of this kind and the belief that we might take a strong
                    line is salutary.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
